# derived corpus: 4.9 MB, exactly reproducible from yarn/make-ladder-ids.py at the
# corpus_commit quoted in every ladder receipt. Never a banked receipt.
ladder-ids.txt

# Per-agent gate BINARIES. The keeper mirrors the box's ~/realgate tree into this directory,
# and once the parallel lanes started pinning their own binary (`qwen4exp_real_gate.agentA`
# and friends, so a shared ./target cannot swap the executable under an in-flight battery)
# the mirror started depositing a 47 MB compiled binary INSIDE the public engine repo.
#
# It got committed on 2026-08-31 and the pre-push public-boundary check caught it: sev3
# `provider_name_aws` at line 255188 of the binary — a compiled executable carries the
# provider strings of the machine it was built for, so a build artifact is a boundary leak
# with no way to review it. It was stripped from the two unpushed commits before they left
# the rig (working copies untouched).
#
# Two things this row is worth stating for:
#  - A build artifact is never a receipt. The receipt is the binary's md5 plus the commit it
#    was built from, which is what the cert lines already carry.
#  - `git commit` commits the whole INDEX, not the paths you just added. Three agents share
#    this checkout, so a file another lane staged rides out on the next lane's commit no
#    matter how carefully that lane staged its own paths. Use `git commit -- <paths>` (or
#    check `git diff --cached --name-only` first) in a shared checkout.
bin/qwen4exp_real_gate.agent*
bin/qwen4exp_gpu_gate.agent*
